<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Railways of Central America</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as IRCA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">International Railways of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> (IRCA) was a United States-owned railway company formed in 1912 that controlled most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s railways as well as lines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Closely linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>, which became a major shareholder, it ran the line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> to Puerto Barrios on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and used its monopoly over freight to dominate the country's export trade for decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-railways-of-central-america.jpg" alt="International Railways of Central America" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferrocarril_del_norte2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The company was the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minor-c-keith">Minor C. Keith</a>, who had bought the Guatemala Central and Guatemala Railway companies and in April 1912 merged the Guatemala Central with the Ocos Railroad. The same year the IRCA acquired the Central El Salvadorian Railway and began building a line from La Union to San Miguel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Its lines were of 3 ft narrow gauge. Keith hoped the network would one day reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> and join North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, but it never went beyond La Union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1929 a connection through Zacapa linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, and the network then spanned over 800 miles. Salvadoran goods could now reach the U.S. through Puerto Barrios and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> in 5.5 days instead of 15 days by ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>. In the 1920s local banana growers paid almost twenty times the rates United Fruit paid to ship their fruit.</p><p>By 1933 the IRCA was almost bankrupt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. In 1936 United Fruit paid off $2.6 million of its debt and took 42.6 per cent of its shares, making it in effect a subsidiary, and the railway came to share United Fruit's poor local reputation. In 1949 the IRCA sued United Fruit, and the New York Supreme Court ordered United Fruit to pay $4.5 million; a 1961 judgement added $4 million more.</p><p>A 1958 agreement arising from the 1954 anti-trust suit of the U.S. Department of Justice ended United Fruit's control. 1957 was the railway's last profitable year: the Atlantic highway to Puerto Barrios forced freight rates down, and the closure in 1964 of United Fruit's Tiquisate plantation took away about 10 per cent of its revenue. In October 1961 O. Roy Chalk's Transportation Corporation of America bought a majority stake, which he sold in April 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the end of 1967 the company had defaulted on Guatemalan government loans and owed workers over $800,000. The government foreclosed on 27 December 1968 and the Guatemalan division became the Ferrocarriles de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> (FEGUA). In 1975 the Salvadoran division merged with the Ferrocarril de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> to form the Ferrocarriles Nacionales de El Salvador (FENADESAL). Its lines became the basis of both national networks, and twelve of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-locomotive">steam locomotives</a> remain in Guatemala and five in El Salvador.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “International Railways of Central America” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the early twentieth century the United Fruit Company, with its railway affiliate <b>International Railways of Central America</b> and banana plantations near the Caribbean and Pacific coasts, became the country’s largest landowner and employer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
